    "q-82254": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82254",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226206/assets/472aa6b0afe20f7c1902446102265f758637c9ec00eb985bf38a658dc92f3fe5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226206/assets/77b59ce5c40514338b0d23a31e7028c7fa0f7343aad018576301fcc4ec441ac1.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-82254-a",
              "type": "math",
